- Logging of invalid spawn token logs only client name (`plan.name`), not token value.

## Logging / PII keywords

Command:

```text
grep -rEn "(console\.log|logger\.|slog\.|fmt\.Print|print\()" internal/kernel cmd/tabula tools/tabula-distro/src examples/plugin-hello examples/plugin-sdk-python --exclude-dir=__pycache__
```

Relevant output summary:

```text
internal/kernel/plugin/runtime.go:258: logger.Info("plugin stderr", "plugin", id, "line", sc.Text())
internal/kernel/plugin_tools.go:168: h.Logger.LogAttrs(context.Background(), level, params.Msg, slog.String("plugin", handle.ID()))
cmd/tabula/main.go:153: slog.Info("running boot", "command", bootCmd)
cmd/tabula/main.go:254: slog.Info("spawning boot process", "command", cmd)
cmd/tabula/main.go:724: slog.Error("boot script stderr", "output", stderr.String())
cmd/tabula/main.go:730: slog.Warn("boot script warnings", "output", stderr.String())
examples/plugin-hello/run.py:51: api.log("hello_child_spawned", child_pid=child.pid)
```

Assessment:

- No direct logging of API keys/passwords/tokens found in audited BUILD changes.
- `plugin_tools.go` currently forwards only plugin log message and plugin id; it intentionally drops plugin-supplied fields, reducing PII leakage risk.
- `plugin/runtime.go` forwards plugin stderr line content. This may include plugin-authored sensitive output if a plugin writes it to stderr; classified as `WARNING`, non-blocking, because plugin authoring docs should warn plugins not to emit secrets and this is a common diagnostic surface.
- Existing boot command/stderr logging can include whatever a local boot script chooses to print; not introduced by PluginRuntime, but still a caution for operators.
